WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.800
Harmonic's Aristotle

00:00:01.612 --> 00:00:04.371
proves a 2,843-frame

00:00:04.194 --> 00:00:05.739
Celeste Classic speedrun

00:00:05.692 --> 00:00:06.611
is optimal

00:00:07.134 --> 00:00:08.572
Harmonic's Aristotle

00:00:08.384 --> 00:00:10.282
translated the PICO-8

00:00:10.094 --> 00:00:11.485
game Celeste Classic

00:00:11.297 --> 00:00:12.369
into Lean and

00:00:12.181 --> 00:00:13.572
formally proved that

00:00:13.384 --> 00:00:14.280
no run can

00:00:14.092 --> 00:00:16.968
beat the 2,843-frame

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:18.030
tool-assisted record

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:19.575
A simple climb

00:00:19.387 --> 00:00:20.212
bound of 3

00:00:20.024 --> 00:00:21.333
pixels per frame

00:00:21.262 --> 00:00:22.417
only proves about

00:00:22.229 --> 00:00:24.057
1,800 frames

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:25.519
so Aristotle had

00:00:25.331 --> 00:00:25.943
to prune the

00:00:25.755 --> 00:00:26.722
search space

00:00:27.254 --> 00:00:28.362
The write-up also

00:00:28.174 --> 00:00:29.588
shows how PICO-8's

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.782
16.16 fixed-point gravity

00:00:31.594 --> 00:00:32.501
differs from the

00:00:32.313 --> 00:00:33.610
floating-point gravity

00:00:33.421 --> 00:00:34.258
in the Celia

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:35.072
TAS tool

00:00:35.332 --> 00:00:36.251
enough to change

00:00:36.063 --> 00:00:37.124
one room's optimal

00:00:36.936 --> 00:00:37.513
time

00:00:38.046 --> 00:00:39.060
Full story on

00:00:38.871 --> 00:00:39.767
the dashboard
